overhanging.", "tags": [], "examples": ["beetle brows"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːtəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯tʰəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯tʰl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiɾəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ɾəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ɾl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiʔəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ʔəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ʔl̩]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "beetles", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "beetling", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "beetled", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "beetled", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(ambitransitive) To loom over; to extend or jut (above).", "tags": ["ambitransitive"], "examples": ["The heavy chimney beetled over the thatched roof."], "quotes": [{"text": "To the dreadful summit of the cliff / That beetles o'er his base into the sea.", "reference": "c. 1599–1602 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Hamlet, Prince of Denmarke”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene iv]:"}, {"text": "Each beetling rampart, and each tower sublime.", "reference": "1822, William Wordsworth, In a Carriage, upon the Banks of the Rhin:"}, {"text": "I was indeed gently affected, and shared his fears, remembering well the bulging walls of the old house, and the toppling mass of heavy chimney work which beetled over the roof, beneath which these poor doves had made their nest.", "reference": "1858 January-March, Dean of Pimlico, “A Story for the New Year”, in Dublin University Magazine reprinted in Littell's Living Age, volume 56 (volume 20 of the second series), Littell, Son & Company, page 63:"}, {"text": "He stood a barehead boy upon a cliff / Pine-crowned, that hung high beetling a bleak north sea, / His long bright hair did stream like yellow silk, […]", "reference": "1871, The Spectator, volume 44, page 519:"}, {"text": "Impelled by some obscure quest, I ascended a rift or cleft in this beetling precipice, noting as I did so the black mouths of many fearsome burrows extending from both walls into the depths of the stony plateau.", "reference": "1941, Chapman Miske, The Thing in the Moonlight:"}, {"text": "By the power of an imperious spirit, Peter was able to extend his meagre five feet two in space by expanding his chest, cocking his crest, inflating his whiskers, and beetling his brows.", "reference": "1950, Norman Lindsay, Dust or Polish?, Sydney: Angus and Robertson, page 69:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːtəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯tʰəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯tʰl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiɾəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ɾəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ɾl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiʔəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ʔəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ʔl̩]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "beetles", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A type of mallet with a large wooden head, used to drive wedges, beat pavements, etc.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "трамбовка"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "puunuija"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "mailloche"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Holzhammer"}, {"language": {"code": "ota", "name": "Ottoman Turkish"}, "word": "طوقماق"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A machine in which fabrics are subjected to a hammering process while passing over rollers, as in cotton mills; a beetling machine.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːtəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯tʰəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯tʰl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiɾəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ɾəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ɾl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiʔəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ʔəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ʔl̩]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "beetles", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "beetling", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "beetled", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "beetled", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To beat with a heavy mallet.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To finish by subjecting to a hammering process in a beetle or beetling machine.", "tags": [], "examples": ["to beetle cotton goods"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiːtəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯tʰəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯tʰl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiɾəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ɾəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ɾl̩]", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈbiʔəl/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈbɪi̯ʔəl] ~ [ˈbɪi̯ʔl̩]", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "beetles", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Archaic spelling of betel.", "tags": ["alt of", "archaic"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Scatter black sand, and offer camphor, sandal, flowers, beetle-leaves, and all sorts of fragrance.", "reference": "1816, John Callaway, Yakkun Nattannawa: A Cingalese Poem, Descriptive of the Ceylon System of Demonology, page 10:"}, {"text": "Beetle-leaf is absolutely a primary necessary article of life; every person consumes it daily, especially when travelling; the hindoo of caste has great difficulties in the way of cooking food, which are augmented whenever from home, and the beetle alleviates his hunger.", "reference": "1835, Alexander's East India and Colonial Magazine: 1835,1/6, page 547:"}, {"text": "\"It consists of the beetle leaf, in which the piece of an areca-nut is mixed with some lime out of the oyster and muscle shells, and so put into the mouth and chewed. Out of the beetle leaves, during the preparation, the fibres that are hard and rough are first drawn to the leaf; the form of a little horn is then given, open at the bottom, and pointed at the top, which when the areca-nut is inserted is closed, and so chewed.\"", "reference": "1835, The Gentleman's Magazine, Volume 157, E. Cave, pages 614–615:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beetle", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}